# Gates Verify USE, Not Shape

## The rule

When you write a gate — a validator check, a test, a golden, an audit — ask one question before
you finish:

> **Would this fire if the thing existed, was perfectly well-formed, and nothing consumed it?**

If the answer is no, the gate verifies **shape**. Shape is the cheap half. Every defect that
survives a green suite lives in the other half: whether anything actually **uses** the thing.

The same rule applies when you write a **contract** (a command file, a pack declaration, a
protocol): a prohibition without a prescribed record is not a contract, it is an invitation to
improvise — and two readers will improvise differently.

## Why this is a rule and not a preference

Measured on 2026-08-23, during one refactor, **five separate defects of this exact shape**, each
past a green validator and a green suite:

| Gate reported green because | The defect was |
|---|---|
| every declaration was individually correct | the ordered SEQUENCE never mentioned the step, so nobody did it |
| the export was well-formed and validated | its resolver had never once been RUN inside it |
| every path reference resolved | a template's placeholders were escaped, so the prose never RENDERED |
| the citation was valid and its target existed | nothing told the reader how to PRODUCE the thing cited |
| the privacy boundary failed closed, and ran twice | it matched file PATHS while the secret sat inside a file |

A sixth, from the contract half: a command said *"report it rather than detect"* and never said what
to **write**. Two agents, the same contract, two different ad-hoc record shapes — one of which
recorded a value every downstream stage would have believed.

## Applying it

**Writing a gate.** Pair every "the thing is present" assertion with a "the thing is reached"
assertion. `pack.rules[]` listed rules for months while nothing read the key; the list was even
wrong (4 of 7) and no one noticed, because being *well-formed* was all anything checked.

**Writing a contract.** Every prohibition names its replacement record. Every citation of a
generated artefact says how to generate it. Every declaration names its consumer, or it is
decoration.

**Writing a projection or generator.** Write the output AFTER everything it interpolates exists.
A projection placed beside its siblings rather than after its inputs renders `undefined`, or
silently falls back to an empty default and states the opposite of the truth.

**Reviewing a green run.** "All checks passed" answers the question the checks were written to ask.
It never answers the question you have in mind. Before trusting it, name one way the system could be
broken that the suite structurally cannot see — then decide whether that way matters.

## The cheapest counter-move

**Run the thing.** Four of the six were found by execution, not inspection: a pipeline run inside an
export, a resolver run inside an export, a differential run of two agents, a brief rendered and read.
A gate is an argument that something is correct; running it is evidence. Prefer evidence, and when
a subject cannot be run, say so plainly rather than letting a structural pass stand in for it.

## Closing a finding — ask what else the argument covers

**A fix that lands on the INSTANCE rather than the CLASS is this framework's dominant recurring
defect.** Measured 2026-09-02/04 across two dogfood runs: **six instances, three different authors,
three different files, inside roughly 72 hours** — and in one case the correct fix sat **ten lines
away in the same JSON object**. "Nobody knew better" is not available as a cause.

| The lesson | Applied to | Not applied to |
|---|---|---|
| a pack may declare an incapacity | `per_tranche_visual_compare` | the smoke leg 10 lines away in the same file |
| a count is forgeable, so record the process | `discovery_mode` records *who answered* | not whether the floor held, nor whether the operator was asked |
| declare the gate you cannot satisfy | the per-tranche compare | every other unsatisfiable gate |
| give `/design` the skills its constraint needs | 2 of the 3 | the one carrying the numbers |
| a success message is not evidence about content | a subagent's `Write` | the author's own heredoc, two hours later |
| **the same trap again** | the gate | **the substitute, which was itself unsupported** |

**Every instance was individually reasonable and complete with respect to the defect that prompted
it.** Nobody decides to fix only the instance. That is the mechanism, not an excuse — and it is why
the counter-move **cannot be a virtue**:

> **"What else does this argument cover?" is a STEP, not a habit.** A habit is exercised when someone
> remembers. A step runs every time.

Proof that memory is insufficient: an agent **writing the section that described this defect
committed it in the same pass** — it corrected two of the three places a false claim appeared and
left the third. It survived the whole analysis and surfaced only through an unrelated grep.

**Before closing any finding, do all four. They are cheap and they are mechanical:**

1. **Grep for the pattern you just corrected**, across the whole file and then the whole tree. Not
   for the *symptom* — for the *shape*. If you fixed a false claim, find every copy of the claim.
2. **Read the neighbours.** A declaration's siblings in the same object, the next key, the parallel
   stage. Co-location is where this defect concentrates, because the author is already looking there.
3. **Ask whether the FIX has the same hole.** If you gave something a fallback, is the fallback
   subject to the condition that made the fallback necessary? That question alone catches the
   substitute-of-an-incapacity case, which no amount of care about the original gate would have.
4. **State the class in the finding**, not just the instance. A finding that names only what broke
   teaches the next reader nothing about where else to look.

When the class genuinely cannot be closed now — a fix that would change the instrument mid-measurement,
or a change needing an operator decision — **say so and record the remainder**. An unclosed class that
is written down is a backlog item; an unclosed class nobody noticed is the next finding.
